# Docker Multi-stage vs Single-stage — Test Project

This project lets you build the **same static site** two different ways and
directly compare the results.

## Files

```
docker-compare/
├── package.json          # build script + a devDependency (eslint) to show bloat
├── build.js               # simulates a real build step (src/ -> dist/)
├── src/
│   ├── index.html
│   ├── style.css
│   └── script.js
├── Dockerfile.multistage  # GOOD: build stage + slim nginx runtime stage
├── Dockerfile.singlestage # BAD (for comparison): everything in one image
├── nginx.conf              # used only by the multi-stage image
├── .dockerignore
└── compare.sh              # builds both, prints sizes, runs both
```

## 1. Build each image individually

```bash
docker build -f Dockerfile.multistage -t demo-multistage:latest .
docker build -f Dockerfile.singlestage -t demo-singlestage:latest .
```

## 2. Compare sizes

```bash
docker images | grep demo-
```

Expect something like:

```
demo-singlestage   latest   ...   ~250-400MB
demo-multistage    latest   ...   ~25-45MB
```

(Exact numbers vary by platform/architecture, but multi-stage should be
roughly 5-10x smaller here, and far more if you add heavier devDependencies.)

## 3. Run each and confirm both work

```bash
docker run -d --rm --name demo-multi -p 8081:8080 demo-multistage:latest
docker run -d --rm --name demo-single -p 8082:8080 demo-singlestage:latest
```

Visit:
- http://localhost:8081 → served by nginx (multi-stage)
- http://localhost:8082 → served by `serve` on Node (single-stage)

Check the health endpoint on the multi-stage image:

```bash
curl http://localhost:8081/healthz
```

Clean up:

```bash
docker stop demo-multi demo-single
```

## 4. Or just run the all-in-one script

```bash
chmod +x compare.sh
./compare.sh
```

## 5. Inspect what's actually inside each image (the real proof)

This is the most convincing check — look at what's inside each image:

```bash
docker run --rm demo-multistage:latest sh -c "which node || echo 'no node in this image'"
docker run --rm demo-singlestage:latest sh -c "which node && echo 'node IS in this image'"
```

The multi-stage image won't even have Node installed at runtime — only
nginx and your static files. The single-stage image carries the entire
Node runtime, npm, eslint, node_modules, and your original source files,
none of which are needed to serve static HTML.
